'''II.''' ''n.'' A yoke-toed bird; a bird having the toes arranged in pairs.

'''Zygodactyla''' (zī-gọ̄-dak′ti-lä̤), ''n. pl.'' [NL. (Brandt, 1835), fem. of *''zygodactylus'': see ''zygodactylous''.] '''1.''' A genus of acalephs, of the family ''Æquoreidæ''. {{smaller|It includes some large jellyfishes, 6 or 8 inches in diameter, with long violet streamers, found in the north Atlantic waters.}}

'''2.''' A section of pachydermatous mammals, corresponding to the ''Suidæ'' in a broad sense; the swine. {{smaller|The name implied the cloven hoof of these animals, in distinction from the solidungulate or multungulate hoof of the quadrupeds with which swine were formerly classed as ''Pachydermata''. See ''Artiodactyla'' (with cut).}}
